# 8. Deploy the Wazuh Agent

The next step is monitoring a Linux endpoint.

The Wazuh agent can collect:

* Authentication logs
* System logs
* File changes
* Security configuration information
* System inventory
* Other endpoint security events

Wazuh provides Linux packages for supported distributions, and the current agent documentation recommends deploying the agent from the official Wazuh repository.

---

# 9. Add the Wazuh Repository

For a Debian/Ubuntu endpoint, install the required packages:

```bash
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y gnupg apt-transport-https curl
```

Import the Wazuh GPG key:

```bash
curl -s https://packages.wazuh.com/key/GPG-KEY-WAZUH | \
sudo gpg --no-default-keyring \
--keyring gnupg-ring:/usr/share/keyrings/wazuh.gpg \
--import
```

Set permissions:

```bash
sudo chmod 644 /usr/share/keyrings/wazuh.gpg
```

Add the repository:

```bash
echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/wazuh.gpg] https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/apt/ stable main" | \
sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/wazuh.list
```

Update:

```bash
sudo apt-get update
```

These are based on the current official Linux agent deployment instructions.

---

# 10. Install the Wazuh Agent

Replace:

```text
WAZUH_MANAGER_IP
```

with the IP address of your Wazuh server.

Example:

```bash
WAZUH_MANAGER="192.168.1.10" sudo apt-get install wazuh-agent
```

The official documentation uses the `WAZUH_MANAGER` deployment variable to specify the Wazuh manager address.

---

# 11. Start the Wazuh Agent

Run:

```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
```

Enable the service:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable wazuh-agent
```

Start it:

```bash
sudo systemctl start wazuh-agent
```

Check:

```bash
sudo systemctl status wazuh-agent
```

Expected concept:

```text
Active: active (running)
```

The official Wazuh Linux agent documentation uses the same enable/start workflow.

