# 18. First Alert Investigation 🕵️

Suppose Wazuh reports:

```text
Alert:
SSH Authentication Failure

Host:
ubuntu-lab

User:
wronguser

Source:
127.0.0.1

Service:
SSH
```

Now perform basic SOC triage.

Ask:

```text
WHO?
WHAT?
WHEN?
WHERE?
WHY?
```

---

# 19. WHO?

Identify the account:

```text
wronguser
```

Was this a valid user?

Check:

```bash
getent passwd wronguser
```

If nothing is returned, the account may not exist.

---

# 20. WHAT?

The event was:

```text
SSH authentication failure
```

This means an authentication attempt failed.

It does NOT automatically mean:

```text
Successful compromise
```

---

# 21. WHEN?

Check the event timestamp.

Example:

```text
10:15:01
10:15:05
10:15:10
```

Multiple failures close together are more interesting than a single isolated failure.

---

# 22. WHERE?

Identify the source IP.

Example:

```text
127.0.0.1
```

In this lab, that is expected because the test was performed against the local machine.

This is an important SOC lesson:

> **Context changes the meaning of an alert.**

A failed SSH login from your own lab machine is very different from an unexpected login attempt from an unknown external address.

---

# 23. WHY?

Ask:

```text
Was this activity expected?
```

In our lab:

```text
Yes
```

because:

```text
We generated the event intentionally.
```

Therefore:

```text
Alert
 ↓
Investigation
 ↓
Known lab activity
 ↓
Benign / expected
```

This is an example of why analysts must investigate alerts instead of blindly treating every alert as an attack.

